\ Slice 13 phase 3 (R-B8): the mutable write witness. `setat` borrows its
\ array local mutably, takes a mutable reference to one element, and stores
\ through it -- generic, and monomorphized here at two distinct types (`i64`
\ then the two-field `Vec2`) so a stride bug in generic element-ref lowering
\ shows up as clobbered neighbour fields, not just a wrong scalar. The borrow
\ is dead once `!` consumes the element reference, so the array can be named
\ and returned afterwards.
import: intrinsics * ;
import: hosted::show | . | ;

type: Vec2 x i64 y i64 ;

: setat['T: Copy] ( array['T 4] 'T -- array['T 4] ) | a v | &!a 2 &!> v ! a ;
: main ( -- )
  0 4 fill | a |
  &!a 0 &!> 10 !  &!a 1 &!> 20 !  &!a 2 &!> 30 !  &!a 3 &!> 40 !
  a 99 setat | b |
  &b 2 &> @ .
  &b 1 &> @ .
  b drop
  0 0 Vec2 4 fill | c |
  &!c 0 &!> 11 12 Vec2 !  &!c 1 &!> 21 22 Vec2 !  &!c 2 &!> 31 32 Vec2 !  &!c 3 &!> 41 42 Vec2 !
  c 91 92 Vec2 setat | d |
  &d 2 &> @ | e | &e &x @ . &e &y @ .
  &d 1 &> @ | e2 | &e2 &x @ . &e2 &y @ .
  d drop ;
